AB  - This paper demonstrates for the first time transformation of a series of 17-oxo steroidal substrates (epiandrosterone, dehydroepiandrosterone, androstenedione) by the most frequently used whole cell biocatalyst, Beauveria bassiana, to 11-hydroxy-17a-oxa-d-homo-androst-17-one products, in the following sequence of reactions: 11-hydroxylation and subsequent Baeyer-Villiger oxidation to a ring-D lactone. 11-Hydroxyprogesterone, the product of the first stage of the progesterone metabolism, was further converted along two routes: hydroxylation to 6,11-dihydroxyprogesterone or 17-acetyl chain degradation leading to 11-hydroxytestosterone, the main metabolite of the substrate. Part of 11-hydroxytestosterone underwent a rare reduction to 11-hydroxy-5-dihydrotestosterone. The experiments have demonstrated that the Baeyer-Villiger monooxygenase produced by the strain catalyzes solely oxidation of C-20 or C-17 ketones with 11-hydroxyl group. 17-Oxo steroids, beside the 11-hydroxylation and Baeyer-Villiger oxidation, also underwent reduction to 17-alcohols; activity of 17-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (17-HSD) has significant impact on the amount of the formed ring-D -lactone.
